    "result_text": "Done. Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\n`locality` is a read-only field derived from an owner's `city`, read back on `GET /api/owners/{id}` \u2014 the same category as the existing derived fields (`displayName`, `membershipNumber`, `membershipTier`). I followed that established convention rather than touching the POST pipeline or persistence:\n\n1. **`model/Locality.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility unit holding the fixed city-to-region table (`Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD`) and one lookup method `forCity(city)` returning the canonical region or `\"UNKNOWN\"`. This keeps the domain table as its own reusable unit instead of embedding it in the mapper.\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `locality(owner)` derivation helper (mirroring `membershipTier`) that delegates to `Locality.forCity(owner.getCity())`, plus the `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", ...)` wiring. The mapper stays thin \u2014 it just wires, the table lives in its own class.\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 declared `locality` as a `readOnly` string on the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\nNo pipeline, persistence, or escalation changes were needed since the value is purely derived from the already-stored `city` at response time.",
